-block-204","→ CPU + device emulation",["UTM","Architecture and core design"]],["UTM/","source-block-248","→ hardware-assisted virtualization on Apple Silicon & Intel Macs",["UTM","Architecture and core design"]],["UTM/","source-block-354","UTM → native macOS GUI around QEMU configs",["UTM","Architecture and core design"]],["UTM/","source-block-403","Two execution modes:",["UTM","Architecture and core design"]],["UTM/","source-block-428","Virtualization (hardware accelerated)",["UTM","Architecture and core design","Two execution modes:"]],["UTM/","source-block-475","Near-native speed",["Architecture and core design","Two execution modes:","Virtualization (hardware accelerated)"]],["UTM/","source-block-499","Requires same CPU architecture as host",["Architecture and core design","Two execution modes:","Virtualization (hardware accelerated)"]],["UTM/","source-block-544","Best for: Linux ARM on Apple Silicon, Windows ARM on Apple Silicon",["Architecture and core design","Two execution modes:","Virtualization (hardware accelerated)"]],["UTM/","source-block-617","Emulation",["UTM","Architecture and core design","Two execution modes:"]],["UTM/","source-block-636","Cross-architecture (e.g., x86 on Apple Silicon)",["Architecture and core design","Two execution modes:","Emulation"]],["UTM/","source-block-690","Slower (full CPU translation)",["Architecture and core design","Two execution modes:","Emulation"]],["UTM/","source-block-726","Useful for legacy systems or testing",["Architecture and core design","Two execution modes:","Emulation"]],["UTM/","source-block-769","UTM is essentially a GUI virtual machine manager built on top of . On macOS it integrates with Apple’s Hypervisor framework for near-native virtualization of ARM64 and CPU/Arch/x86 guests when the host CPU is compatible, falling back to QEMU’s JIT or interpreter for cross-architecture emulation (for example, x86 on Apple silicon).",["UTM","Architecture and core design"]],["UTM/","source-block-1132","This design lets users pick between speed (virtualization) and flexibility (emulation), while UTM’s UI generates and manages the underlying QEMU configurations, so users typically don’t have to hand-craft QEMU command lines.",["UTM","Architecture and core design"]],["UTM/","source-block-1365","Key facts",["UTM"]],["UTM/","source-block-1382","Type: manager / system emulator",["UTM","Key facts"]],["UTM/","source-block-1444","Developer: Turing Software, LLC",["UTM","Key facts"]],["UTM/","source-block-1485","Platforms: macOS, iOS, visionOS on Intel and Apple silicon hardware",["UTM","Key facts"]],["UTM/","source-block-1562","License: Apache License 2.0 (open source)",["UTM","Key facts"]],["UTM/","source-block-1613","Distribution: Free builds via GitHub; paid convenience build on Mac App Store",["UTM","Key facts"]],["uv/","source-block-0","astral-sh/uv: An extremely fast Python package and project manager, written in Rust.",[]],["uv/","source-block-136","Recommended by Person/Sebastian Raschka",["astral-sh/uv: An extremely fast Python package and project manager, written in Rust."]],["uv/concept/python-versions/","source-block-29","Python versions | uv",[]],["uv/How%20To/Add%20a%20Package%20to%20pyproject.toml/","source-block-83","How To Add a Package to pyproject.toml with uv",[]],["uv/How%20To/Add%20a%20Package%20to%20pyproject.toml/","source-block-134","Overview",["How To Add a Package to pyproject.toml with uv"]],["uv/How%20To/Add%20a%20Package%20to%20pyproject.toml/","source-block-149","This guide walks you through adding a package dependency to your Python project's pyproject.toml file using the uv tool.",["How To Add a Package to pyproject.toml with uv","Overview"]],["uv/How%20To/Add%20a%20Package%20to%20pyproject.toml/","source-block-274","It's for Python developers who are working on projects managed with uv and need to add new dependencies.",["How To Add a Package to pyproject.toml with uv","Overview"]],["uv/How%20To/Add%20a%20Package%20to%20pyproject.toml/","source-block